            <fr:title text="iter_args and yield are the mutable accumulator">iter_args and yield are the mutable accumulator</fr:title>
            <fr:taxon>Card</fr:taxon>
            <fr:meta name="anki">1787404869520</fr:meta>
            <fr:meta name="anki-deck">MLSys</fr:meta>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
  <html:p>Read this loop: <html:code><![CDATA[%r = scf.for %i = %c0 to %c8 step %c1 iter_args(%acc = %init) -> (f32) { %n = arith.addf %acc, %x : f32; scf.yield %n : f32 }]]></html:code></html:p>
  
  
  
 <html:div class="jms-callout" style="
  margin: 1.1rem 0;
  padding: 0.1rem 0 0.1rem 0.9rem;
  border-left: 2px solid rgb(63, 83, 127);
  "><html:span style="font-weight: 500; color: rgb(63, 83, 127);" /> 
  <html:details><html:summary><html:span class="foldout" style="font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 11pt; font-weight: 500; color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.65);">Answer</html:span></html:summary>
  A structured loop that carries state.

  <html:ul><html:li><html:code><![CDATA[%i]]></html:code> is a block argument of the loop body (<fr:link href="/QZFU/" title="Block arguments replace phi nodes" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/QZFU/" display-uri="QZFU" type="local">Block arguments replace phi nodes</fr:link>), not a value from the enclosing scope.</html:li>
    <html:li><html:code><![CDATA[iter_args(%acc = %init)]]></html:code> declares the loop-carried values and initialises them once, before the first iteration.</html:li>
    <html:li><html:code>scf.yield</html:code> supplies the next iteration's <html:code>iter_args</html:code> and, on the final iteration, the loop's results <html:code><![CDATA[%r]]></html:code>.</html:li></html:ul>

  <html:strong>So <html:code>iter_args</html:code> plus <html:code>scf.yield</html:code> is the functional spelling of a mutable accumulator.</html:strong> Nothing is stored; each iteration produces a new value. That is what keeps the loop in <fr:link href="/4SV9/" title="Static single assignment" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/4SV9/" display-uri="4SV9" type="local">Static single assignment</fr:link> form and lets it be analysed and rewritten as a pure region (<fr:link href="/MKYF/" title="A region is nesting, not a flat CFG" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/MKYF/" display-uri="MKYF" type="local">A region is nesting, not a flat CFG</fr:link>).

  Both the jaxpr <html:code>scan</html:code> (<fr:link href="/W8NM/" title="consts, then carry, then xs" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/W8NM/" display-uri="W8NM" type="local">consts, then carry, then xs</fr:link>) and the HLO <html:code>while</html:code> (<fr:link href="/YXTI/" title="A while loop's whole state is one tuple" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/YXTI/" display-uri="YXTI" type="local">A while loop's whole state is one tuple</fr:link>) use the same idea — the carry is explicit and typed — differing only in how the carried values are packed.
</html:details>
</html:div>



</fr:mainmatter>
