            <fr:title text="Symbols are looked up, values are defined">Symbols are looked up, values are defined</fr:title>
            <fr:taxon>Card</fr:taxon>
            <fr:meta name="anki">1787404866520</fr:meta>
            <fr:meta name="anki-deck">MLSys</fr:meta>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
  <html:p>In <fr:link href="/IR7B/" title="MLIR" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/IR7B/" display-uri="IR7B" type="local">MLIR</fr:link>, what is <html:code>@foo</html:code> versus <html:code><![CDATA[%foo]]></html:code>?</html:p>
  
  
  
 <html:div class="jms-callout" style="
  margin: 1.1rem 0;
  padding: 0.1rem 0 0.1rem 0.9rem;
  border-left: 2px solid rgb(63, 83, 127);
  "><html:span style="font-weight: 500; color: rgb(63, 83, 127);" /> 
  <html:details><html:summary><html:span class="foldout" style="font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 11pt; font-weight: 500; color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.65);">Answer</html:span></html:summary>
  <html:code>@foo</html:code> is a <html:em>symbol</html:em> reference — a name resolved by lookup in a SymbolTable (a <html:code>func.func</html:code>, a global). <html:code><![CDATA[%foo]]></html:code> is an <fr:link href="/4SV9/" title="Static single assignment" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/4SV9/" display-uri="4SV9" type="local">Static single assignment</fr:link> value defined earlier in the same region.

  Nested symbols use <html:code>@parent::@child</html:code>.

  The distinction shows up in the type list, which is the tell when reading a call:

  <html:pre><html:code class="language-llvm"><![CDATA[func.call @foo(%a) : (i32) -> i32]]></html:code></html:pre>

  The callee is a symbol, not an operand, so it does <html:em>not</html:em> appear in the operand types — only <html:code><![CDATA[%a]]></html:code> does. A symbol is resolved at compile time; a value flows at runtime.
</html:details>
</html:div>



</fr:mainmatter>
        </fr:tree>
