            <fr:title text="Block arguments replace phi nodes">Block arguments replace phi nodes</fr:title>
            <fr:taxon>Card</fr:taxon>
            <fr:meta name="anki">1787404869720</fr:meta>
            <fr:meta name="anki-deck">MLSys</fr:meta>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
  <html:p>Read this line: <html:code><![CDATA[^bb1(%arg0: i32, %arg1: f32):]]></html:code></html:p>
  
  
  
 <html:div class="jms-callout" style="
  margin: 1.1rem 0;
  padding: 0.1rem 0 0.1rem 0.9rem;
  border-left: 2px solid rgb(63, 83, 127);
  "><html:span style="font-weight: 500; color: rgb(63, 83, 127);" /> 
  <html:details><html:summary><html:span class="foldout" style="font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 11pt; font-weight: 500; color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.65);">Answer</html:span></html:summary>
  A block label with <html:em>block arguments</html:em>. <fr:link href="/IR7B/" title="MLIR" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/IR7B/" display-uri="IR7B" type="local">MLIR</fr:link> has no phi nodes — instead each predecessor passes the merged values as branch operands:

  <html:pre><html:code class="language-llvm"><![CDATA[cf.br ^bb1(%x, %y : i32, f32)]]></html:code></html:pre>

  Block arguments <html:em>are</html:em> the <fr:link href="/4SV9/" title="Static single assignment" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/4SV9/" display-uri="4SV9" type="local">Static single assignment</fr:link> merge mechanism. The trade is that a value's incoming edges are recorded at the branches rather than collected at the merge, which makes CFG edits local: rewriting one predecessor touches one op.

  The entry block of a function-like op takes the function's parameters this same way, which is why <html:code><![CDATA[%arg0]]></html:code> is the conventional name for a function argument.
</html:details>
</html:div>



</fr:mainmatter>
        </fr:tree>
        <fr:tree show-metadata="true" expanded="false" toc="false" numbered="false">
          <fr:frontmatter>
            <fr:authors>
              <fr:author>
                <fr:link href="/kellenkanarios/" title="Kellen Kanarios" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/kellenkanarios/" display-uri="kellenkanarios" type="local">Kellen Kanarios</fr:link>
              </fr:author>
            </fr:authors>
            <fr:date>
              <fr:year>2026</fr:year>
              <fr:month>8</fr:month>
              <fr:day>22</fr:day>
            </fr:date>
            <fr:uri>https://kellenkanarios.com/MOVC/</fr:uri>
            <fr:display-uri>MOVC</fr:display-uri>
            <fr:route>/MOVC/</fr:route>
            <fr:title text="SSA names are printing hints"><fr:link href="/4SV9/" title="Static single assignment" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/4SV9/" display-uri="4SV9" type="local">SSA</fr:link> names are printing hints</fr:title>
            <fr:taxon>Card</fr:taxon>
            <fr:meta name="anki">1787404869371</fr:meta>
            <fr:meta name="anki-deck">MLSys</fr:meta>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
  <html:p>Are <fr:link href="/IR7B/" title="MLIR" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/IR7B/" display-uri="IR7B" type="local">MLIR</fr:link> value names like <html:code><![CDATA[%arg0]]></html:code>, <html:code><![CDATA[%c0_i32]]></html:code>, <html:code><![CDATA[%lhs]]></html:code> semantically meaningful?</html:p>
  
  
  
 <html:div class="jms-callout" style="
  margin: 1.1rem 0;
  padding: 0.1rem 0 0.1rem 0.9rem;
  border-left: 2px solid rgb(63, 83, 127);
  "><html:span style="font-weight: 500; color: rgb(63, 83, 127);" /> 
  <html:details><html:summary><html:span class="foldout" style="font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 11pt; font-weight: 500; color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.65);">Answer</html:span></html:summary>
  <html:strong>No.</html:strong> They are printing hints only. Internally <fr:link href="/4SV9/" title="Static single assignment" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/4SV9/" display-uri="4SV9" type="local">Static single assignment</fr:link> values are positional and unnamed; the asm printer reconstructs a name from a name-hint interface, which is why constants come out as <html:code><![CDATA[%c0_i32]]></html:code> or <html:code><![CDATA[%cst]]></html:code> and block arguments as <html:code><![CDATA[%arg0]]></html:code>.

  Two things follow, and both bite when reading dumps:

  <html:ul><html:li><html:code><![CDATA[%arg0]]></html:code> in two different functions or regions are unrelated. The name is scoped to its region and carries no identity across one.</html:li>
    <html:li>The numbering is not stable across passes. Diffing two per-pass dumps (<fr:link href="/8U47/" title="Per-pass dumps find the pass that broke it" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/8U47/" display-uri="8U47" type="local">Per-pass dumps find the pass that broke it</fr:link>) on value names produces noise; diff on structure instead.</html:li></html:ul>

  The <fr:link href="/NRD2/" title="Locations are the provenance of an op" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/NRD2/" display-uri="NRD2" type="local">Locations are the provenance of an op</fr:link> is the stable thing to key on — it survives the passes that renumber values.
</html:details>
</html:div>



</fr:mainmatter>
        </fr:tree>
        <fr:tree show-metadata="true" expanded="false" toc="false" numbered="false">
          <fr:frontmatter>
            <fr:authors>
              <fr:author>
