            <fr:display-uri>JE7W</fr:display-uri>
            <fr:route>/JE7W/</fr:route>
            <fr:title text="One shape for every op">One shape for every op</fr:title>
            <fr:taxon>Card</fr:taxon>
            <fr:meta name="anki">1787404868545</fr:meta>
            <fr:meta name="anki-deck">MLSys</fr:meta>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
  <html:p>In <fr:link href="/IR7B/" title="MLIR" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/IR7B/" display-uri="IR7B" type="local">MLIR</fr:link>'s generic form, what does each part of <html:code><![CDATA[%0 = "foo.add"(%a, %b) : (i32, i32) -> i32]]></html:code> mean?</html:p>
  
  
  
 <html:div class="jms-callout" style="
  margin: 1.1rem 0;
  padding: 0.1rem 0 0.1rem 0.9rem;
  border-left: 2px solid rgb(63, 83, 127);
  "><html:span style="font-weight: 500; color: rgb(63, 83, 127);" /> 
  <html:details><html:summary><html:span class="foldout" style="font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 11pt; font-weight: 500; color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.65);">Answer</html:span></html:summary>
  Every op, in every dialect, prints to one shape:

  <html:pre><html:code class="language-llvm"><![CDATA[results = "dialect.opname"(operands) ({regions}) {attributes} : (operand types) -> result types]]></html:code></html:pre>

  <html:ul><html:li>The quoted name is always <html:code>dialect.op</html:code>. The prefix is what makes an unfamiliar op findable — it names the dialect whose docs define it.</html:li>
    <html:li>The type after the colon is a <html:em>function type</html:em>: operand types before the arrow, result types after. So the signature is always readable off the one line.</html:li>
    <html:li>Only operands sit in the parens. Attributes are the brace dictionary and are compile-time constants, not <fr:link href="/4SV9/" title="Static single assignment" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/4SV9/" display-uri="4SV9" type="local">Static single assignment</fr:link> values — that split is exactly what is known at compile time versus what flows at runtime.</html:li></html:ul>

  Learn this shape once and any dialect is parseable; the pretty forms are sugar over it (<fr:link href="/T1YS/" title="The pretty form is optional, the generic form always works" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/T1YS/" display-uri="T1YS" type="local">The pretty form is optional, the generic form always works</fr:link>).
</html:details>
</html:div>



</fr:mainmatter>
